(window.HALCYON_MOCK=window.HALCYON_MOCK||[]).push({ part:`Pe2 People`, form:2, formName:`Practice exam 2`,
questions:[

{ q:`Two IT departments have merged and now share one project to build a single service desk. Each group still talks about "our tools" and "their process," and planning meetings keep stalling. What should the project manager do first?`,
  opts:[`Run a joint session to agree on what the new service desk is for`,
        `Adopt the larger department's tools as the standard for everyone`,
        `Ask the sponsor to send both departments a memo stating the vision`,
        `Let each group keep its own tools and process until go live`],
  a:0, why:`A shared vision has to be built with the people who'll carry it, so the project manager brings both groups together to agree on what the merged service desk is for and how it should work for its users. The sponsor's memo is the near miss. Sponsor backing helps, but a vision handed down doesn't settle the split between "ours" and "theirs." Picking one group's tools takes a side, and waiting until go live lets the split harden.`,
  task:`P1`, domain:`People`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`An agile team and its product owner share a clear product vision. Three months in, the sales and support staff who talk to customers every day still describe the product as the old system with a new look. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Email the vision statement to sales and support`,
        `Have the product owner correct each wrong description`,
        `Walk sales and support through the vision at each release`,
        `Leave customer messaging to the marketing team`],
  a:2, why:`Promoting a vision means carrying it beyond the team to the people who speak for the product, and repeating it as the product changes. Walking sales and support through it at each release ties the words to what customers can actually see. Emailing the statement is the near miss. It shares the right words, but a statement alone rarely changes how people talk about a product. Correcting each description one at a time never ends, and handing it to marketing leaves out the people closest to customers.`,
  task:`P1`, domain:`People`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`Six months into building a new school, the sponsor retires. The new sponsor talks about success in terms of community use after hours, which the charter never mentions. What should the project manager do first?`,
  opts:[`Keep executing the approved charter as written`,
        `Review the charter objectives with the new sponsor`,
        `Raise a change request to add community use features`,
        `Brief the team on the new sponsor's priorities`],
  a:1, why:`A new sponsor may see the project differently, and the objectives only work as a common vision if the sponsor shares them. The project manager walks through the charter's objectives with them, confirms or updates them, and then tells the team. The change request is the near miss. It may come later, but it's premature before anyone knows whether the sponsor wants new scope or reads the same goals another way. Carrying on ignores the gap, and briefing the team first spreads priorities nobody has agreed.`,
  task:`P1`, domain:`People`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`At the kickoff of an agile project, a developer asks, "How will we know if this worked?" Stakeholders give answers ranging from "it ships" to "customers stop calling us." What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Use the release date as the main measure of success`,
        `Let the product owner judge success after the first release`,
        `Ask each stakeholder to write down a personal success measure`,
        `Work with stakeholders to set outcomes the team can measure`],
  a:3, why:`A common vision needs one shared, measurable picture of success, built with stakeholders and kept where the team can see it, for example a target for fewer customer calls. Letting the product owner judge later is the near miss. The product owner owns the product's value, but deciding after a release leaves the team building without a target. A ship date measures output, not outcome, and separate personal measures keep the answers scattered.`,
  task:`P1`, domain:`People`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`Halfway through a hybrid project, a contracted team of testers joins. They see only their ticket queue and have never heard why the system is being built. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Send them the charter and business case to read`,
        `Have their own lead pass the project goals on to them`,
        `Keep them focused on the ticket queue for now`,
        `Have them hear from the system's real users`],
  a:3, why:`People share a vision when they understand the problem it solves, and hearing it from the people affected does that faster than documents. Sending the charter and business case is the near miss. The documents are right, but reading them alone rarely builds a shared picture. Passing it through their lead loses detail along the way, and keeping them on tickets treats them as outsiders to the project's purpose.`,
  task:`P1`, domain:`People`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`A city plans to merge three library branches into one new building. In early meetings, staff picture a quiet study hall, the council pictures a community center, and the library board pictures a digital media lab. What should the project manager do before detailed planning?`,
  opts:[`Ask the architect for a building that offers all three things`,
        `Let the library board choose the direction for everyone else`,
        `Help them agree on one future state for the building`,
        `Start the design with the option that has the most support`],
  a:2, why:`Before detailed planning, the project manager helps stakeholders agree on one picture of the future state and writes it down, where it can guide scope. Designing for all three is the near miss. It sounds generous, but without agreed priorities it drives up cost and pleases nobody. Letting one group decide skips the others who have to back it, and going with the most popular option is a vote, not an agreement.`,
  task:`P1`, domain:`People`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A project manager drafts a vision statement alone and emails it to the team and key stakeholders. The replies show people read it in very different ways. What should the project manager do next?`,
  opts:[`Rewrite the statement and send it again`,
        `Ask the sponsor to approve the statement as written`,
        `Hold a working session to shape the statement together`,
        `Answer each reply separately to explain the intent`],
  a:2, why:`The different readings show the vision was handed out rather than built. A working session lets people shape the words and hear each other, and that's what makes it shared. Rewriting it more precisely is the near miss. Clearer wording helps, but it's still one person's view sent out for others to accept. Sponsor approval locks the gap in, and separate replies give everyone a different explanation.`,
  task:`P1`, domain:`People`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`Every sprint, a tester and a developer argue in the review over whether defects found on the last day should block the release. The argument always ends with no decision. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Let the product owner rule on each late defect as it comes up`,
        `Ask the tester to move late defects into the next sprint`,
        `Tell the two of them to settle it before the next review`,
        `Help the team set a rule for late defects at the retrospective`],
  a:3, why:`The conflict repeats because the team has no shared rule. Working it out at the retrospective treats it as a team problem and produces a working agreement both people helped write. Having the product owner rule each time is the near miss. The product owner does decide what ships, but case by case rulings keep the argument alive every sprint. Pushing every late defect to the next sprint takes the developer's side, and leaving the two to settle it alone keeps the rest of the team out of a team question.`,
  task:`P2`, domain:`People`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`Two department heads with equal authority each want the project's test site for the same two weeks before a fixed inspection date. Several meetings haven't found a way for both to get all they want, and the schedule has to be set tomorrow. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Postpone the decision until after the inspection`,
        `Ask the sponsor to give the site to one department`,
        `Have each department give up part of its window`,
        `Stress the goals the two departments share`],
  a:2, why:`When both sides have equal power, collaboration has already been tried and a decision is due now, compromise fits. Each department gives up part of its window, so both get some test time and the schedule can be set. Going to the sponsor is the near miss. The sponsor could decide, but that's forcing from above, it leaves one department with nothing, and it isn't needed while a shared answer is still on the table. Postponing runs past the deadline, and stressing shared goals smooths things over without settling who gets the site.`,
  task:`P2`, domain:`People`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`Two leads disagree about the order of slides in a one-time internal briefing that's due this afternoon. One cares a great deal about it. The other admits they don't mind much. Which way of handling the conflict fits best?`,
  opts:[`Collaborate until the two leads fully agree`,
        `Accommodate the lead who cares more`,
        `Make the call yourself and move on`,
        `Split the difference with a mixed order`],
  a:1, why:`When the issue is minor, time is short, and it matters far more to one side, accommodating, also called smoothing, is the fitting mode. It costs little and keeps the relationship. Collaborating is the near miss. It's often the best mode, but it takes time and energy that a one-time briefing doesn't justify. Forcing a call isn't needed when one side already doesn't mind, and splitting the difference may leave a muddled briefing that pleases neither.`,
  task:`P2`, domain:`People`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`Two weeks ago, the hardware lead and the software lead on a hybrid project settled a long dispute about late interface specs by agreeing to a new handoff checklist. Neither has raised the matter since. What should the project manager do now?`,
  opts:[`Treat it as settled, since neither lead has complained`,
        `Check with both leads whether the checklist is working`,
        `Ask the sponsor to add the checklist to the charter`,
        `Record the dispute in the lessons learned and close it`],
  a:1, why:`Resolving a conflict includes checking that the agreed fix holds. A quick check with both leads shows whether the checklist is being used and whether specs now arrive on time, before the old friction comes back. Recording it in the lessons learned is the near miss. Capturing what worked is useful, but closing the matter before anyone knows the fix works is premature. Silence doesn't prove the problem is gone, and putting a working checklist in the charter adds formality nobody asked for.`,
  task:`P2`, domain:`People`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`Two engineers keep clashing over coding standards. In a private conversation, the project manager learns one of them is still upset about being passed over for the team lead role, which went to the other. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Settle the coding standards question with a team vote`,
        `Address that engineer's disappointment about the lead role`,
        `Assign the two engineers to separate parts of the system`,
        `Ask the new team lead to set the coding standards alone`],
  a:1, why:`The coding standards are the surface. The source is the disappointment about the role, and the conflict won't stop until that's dealt with, for example by talking about growth paths. A team vote on standards is the near miss. It settles the visible dispute fairly, but the next topic will spark the same clash. Separating them avoids the problem, and handing the standards to the new lead deepens the resentment.`,
  task:`P2`, domain:`People`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`In the utility app case, what should the project manager do about the two developers who've stopped reviewing each other's code?`,
  opts:[`Give each of them a different code reviewer`,
        `Meet the two together to work out the disagreement`,
        `Ask each company's manager to talk to its developer`,
        `Raise the skipped reviews at the next retrospective`],
  a:1, why:`The conflict is now hurting quality, so it can't wait. Bringing the two together to understand the disagreement and agree how they'll work treats it as a shared problem, which is the collaborative approach. Raising it at the retrospective is the near miss. The team should talk about review practice, but airing a personal dispute in front of everyone before the two have talked can make it worse. Different reviewers avoid the conflict, and going to each company's manager splits it along company lines.`,
  task:`P2`, domain:`People`, approach:`agile`, case:`m2c1`, cq:5 },

{ q:`Engineers from a partner company agree with plans in meetings but raise objections later by email to their own managers. The project manager suspects a cultural difference but isn't sure. What should the project manager do first?`,
  opts:[`Ask the partner's engineers to object in meetings from now on`,
        `Send each meeting decision to their managers for sign off`,
        `Ask the partner's lead how concerns are usually raised there`,
        `Treat silence in meetings as agreement and move ahead`],
  a:2, why:`Before choosing a fix, the project manager needs to understand the context of the conflict. Asking the partner's lead how disagreement is normally raised in their workplace shows whether this is culture, hierarchy or something else, and points to a way of working that fits both sides. Sending decisions to their managers for sign off is the near miss. It catches objections, but it slows every decision and builds a fix on a guess. Asking them to object in meetings assumes the cause, and treating silence as agreement guarantees more surprises.`,
  task:`P2`, domain:`People`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`On an agile team, a senior developer has started telling others which stories to take during the daily standup. Two members say privately that they feel sidelined. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Make the senior developer the team's official technical lead`,
        `Assign the stories to members in sprint planning yourself`,
        `Ask the two members to raise it at the next standup`,
        `Talk with the senior developer about how work gets picked`],
  a:3, why:`Self-organizing teams choose their own work, and a strong voice can crowd that out without meaning to. A private conversation with the senior developer about their influence protects the team's way of working. Asking the two to raise it is the near miss. Open discussion is healthy, but it puts the burden on the people who already feel sidelined. An official title makes the behavior formal, and assigning stories yourself replaces one director with another.`,
  task:`P3`, domain:`People`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`A project manager takes on a team of new hires for a predictive project. They're eager but have never done this kind of work, and the tasks are detailed and unfamiliar. Which leadership approach fits best at first?`,
  opts:[`Delegate the work and step well back`,
        `Coach them through decisions they make`,
        `Give clear direction and check in often`,
        `Let the team set its own roles and methods`],
  a:2, why:`Situational leadership matches the style to the team's readiness. People who are keen but inexperienced need clear direction and close support at first, then more freedom as they grow. Coaching is the near miss. It's the next stage, once they have some skill, but asking new hires to make unfamiliar decisions now sets them up to struggle. Delegating and full self-organization assume experience they don't have yet.`,
  task:`P3`, domain:`People`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A team member who uses a screen reader has gone quiet in planning sessions since the team moved to a new visual whiteboard tool. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Work with the member and team on an accessible way to plan`,
        `Send the member a written summary after each session`,
        `Ask the tool's vendor to improve its accessibility`,
        `Pair the member with a colleague who can describe the board`],
  a:0, why:`Inclusion means everyone can take part in the work itself, so the project manager works with the member and the team on tools or formats that let them contribute in real time. A written summary is the near miss. It keeps them informed, but after the fact, so their ideas still don't shape the plan. Asking the vendor may help someday, not now, and a describer makes them depend on someone else.`,
  task:`P3`, domain:`People`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`An agile team wants to try mob programming on a hard integration problem. A department manager tells the project manager it looks like five people doing one person's job. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Back the trial and agree to share what it shows`,
        `Ask the team to work alone until the manager agrees`,
        `Limit the mob to two people to keep the cost down`,
        `Let the manager decide whether the trial goes ahead`],
  a:0, why:`A servant leader backs the team's choice of how to work and makes the results visible, for example cycle time and defects, so the manager can judge it on evidence. Limiting it to two people is the near miss. Pairing is a reasonable compromise, but it changes the experiment the team chose before anyone has seen results. Waiting for agreement, or handing the call to the manager, takes the team's way of working out of the team's hands.`,
  task:`P3`, domain:`People`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`A predictive project's team finishes a hard milestone after weeks of steady effort. The project still has a year to go, and energy has been low. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Recognize the people and work behind the milestone`,
        `Plan a bonus for everyone at the end of the project`,
        `Tell the sponsor about the milestone in the next report`,
        `Give everyone on the team the same gift card as thanks`],
  a:0, why:`Timely, specific recognition motivates more than a distant reward, and it shows people their own effort was seen. Ideally it's given the way each person prefers. The same gift card for everyone is the near miss. It's prompt, but it's generic and says nothing about what anyone did. A bonus a year away is too far off to lift energy now, and a line in the report reaches the sponsor, not the team.`,
  task:`P3`, domain:`People`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A respected technician with no formal title tells coworkers the project's new inspection process is pointless, and several of them stop following it. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Hear the technician's objections and ask for their help`,
        `Report the technician to their functional manager`,
        `Retrain the coworkers on the inspection process`,
        `Ask the sponsor to announce that the process is mandatory`],
  a:0, why:`Informal leaders shape what a team does. Hearing the technician's objections, and inviting them to help improve or champion the process, uses that influence instead of fighting it. Retraining is the near miss. The coworkers may need it, but they stopped because of the technician, not for lack of skill. A report or a mandate may get compliance on paper while the doubt keeps spreading.`,
  task:`P3`, domain:`People`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`After a production outage, the team's retrospective is short and quiet. The project manager later hears that people are afraid to admit mistakes. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Run a blameless review and admit a mistake of your own`,
        `Ask the team lead to find out who caused the outage`,
        `Collect anonymous reports about what went wrong in the outage`,
        `Leave the topic until the team feels ready to talk`],
  a:0, why:`People admit mistakes when it's safe to. A blameless review looks at the system, not the person, and a leader who names their own mistake first shows that it's safe. Anonymous reports are the near miss. They gather facts, but they treat the fear as permanent rather than dealing with it. Hunting for who caused the outage confirms the fear, and leaving the topic means the lessons are lost.`,
  task:`P3`, domain:`People`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`A junior employee tells the project manager that a contractor on the team dismisses their ideas in meetings and talks over them. Other team members have noticed it too. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Ask the contractor's firm to send a replacement contractor`,
        `Speak with the contractor privately about the behavior`,
        `Remind everyone of the meeting ground rules`,
        `Coach the junior employee to speak more firmly`],
  a:1, why:`The project manager sets the standard of respect on the team, contractors included, and starts with a private, direct talk about the specific behavior and its effect. Reminding everyone of the ground rules is the near miss. It may help, but it avoids naming a pattern people have already seen. Asking for a replacement is premature before a direct conversation, and coaching the junior employee puts the burden on the person being talked over.`,
  task:`P3`, domain:`People`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`The project manager is updating the engagement plan for a hybrid rollout of a new inventory system. Using the assessment, where should the most engagement effort go first?`,
  fig:`<prop type="doc" title="Stakeholder engagement assessment (levels: unaware, resistant, neutral, supportive, leading)"><table><tr><th>Stakeholder</th><th>Current</th><th>Desired</th></tr><tr><th>Finance director</th><td>Neutral</td><td>Supportive</td></tr><tr><th>Head of operations</th><td>Resistant</td><td>Leading</td></tr><tr><th>IT security lead</th><td>Supportive</td><td>Supportive</td></tr><tr><th>Regional managers</th><td>Unaware</td><td>Neutral</td></tr></table></prop>`,
  opts:[`The regional managers`,
        `The finance director`,
        `The head of operations`,
        `The IT security lead`],
  a:2, why:`The head of operations has the widest gap: resistant now, and the plan needs them leading. Closing that gap takes the most work and matters most for adoption. The regional managers are the near miss. They have a gap too, from unaware to neutral, but informing people is a smaller task than turning resistance into leadership. The finance director needs one step, and the IT security lead is already where they need to be.`,
  task:`P4`, domain:`People`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`In the utility app case, what should the project manager do about the state agency's offer?`,
  opts:[`Wait until the app is finished, then send the layouts`,
        `Decline it, since the team already knows what the rule says`,
        `Accept it and set up layout reviews with the agency's staff`,
        `Ask the utility's lawyers to answer on the project's behalf`],
  a:2, why:`The agency enforces the rule, so it's a high-influence stakeholder, and its offer is a chance to build trust and catch misreadings while they're cheap to fix. Accepting it and planning reviews of draft layouts engages the agency early. Sending the finished layouts is the near miss. The agency still sees them, but too late to shape the work if something's wrong. Declining wastes the chance to check the team's reading of the rule, and routing it through the lawyers turns an offer of help into a formal exchange.`,
  task:`P4`, domain:`People`, approach:`agile`, case:`m2c1`, cq:4 },

{ q:`The engagement plan for a road project calls for monthly briefings to the city council. Even so, council members keep saying they're surprised by project decisions. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Add more detail to each of the monthly council briefings`,
        `Move the briefings from monthly to weekly`,
        `Ask the council members what the briefings are missing`,
        `Send council members the full meeting minutes`],
  a:2, why:`An engagement strategy has to be checked against how stakeholders actually experience it. The surprise is a sign it isn't working, and asking the council members finds out why, perhaps the timing, the format, or who attends. Weekly briefings are the near miss. More contact might help, but changing the frequency before knowing the cause may just mean more briefings that miss. More detail and full minutes guess at the problem the same way.`,
  task:`P4`, domain:`People`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`An agile team is building an internal expense tool. Its sprint reviews are attended only by department managers, and the staff who'll file expenses every week haven't seen it yet. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Have the managers demo it to their staff`,
        `Invite some of the staff who'll use it to the reviews`,
        `Survey the staff once the tool has been released`,
        `Have the product owner speak for the staff`],
  a:1, why:`The people who'll use a product every day are key stakeholders, and engaging them early is how a team learns what works. Bringing some of them to reviews gives direct feedback while it can still shape the product. Having managers pass on the demo is the near miss. It reaches the staff, but the feedback comes back filtered and late. A survey after release is too late, and the product owner can't replace hearing from users directly.`,
  task:`P4`, domain:`People`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`The company's legal counsel can delay every contract the project needs and has said in a meeting that the project is "a liability waiting to happen." What should the project manager do first?`,
  opts:[`Ask the sponsor to overrule the counsel's objections`,
        `Meet the counsel to understand the concerns`,
        `Route the project's contracts through a different reviewer`,
        `Send the counsel a copy of the project's risk register`],
  a:1, why:`A high-influence stakeholder who opposes the project needs early, direct engagement. Understanding the specific concerns is the first step to addressing them, and the counsel may be pointing at real risks. Sending the risk register is the near miss. It shares useful information, but a document won't tell the project manager what worries the counsel or show that anyone listened. Overruling or routing around them makes an influential opponent stronger.`,
  task:`P4`, domain:`People`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A project manager inherits a hybrid project whose stakeholder register was last updated a year ago. Since then there's been a reorganization. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Review and update the register and its analysis now`,
        `Fix entries in the old register as problems arise`,
        `Ask the team to note any new names they hear`,
        `Build a fresh register at the next phase gate`],
  a:0, why:`Identifying stakeholders is ongoing work, and a reorganization changes who has power, interest and a stake. The register and its analysis need updating now, before the engagement plan works from stale names. A fresh register at the next gate is the near miss. Refreshing at a gate is good practice, but waiting means months of engaging the wrong people. Fixing entries as problems arise and collecting names informally both react instead of plan.`,
  task:`P4`, domain:`People`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`A department head who opposed an agile project now attends every sprint review and raises new objections each time, which takes up most of the session. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Ask the department head to send objections in writing`,
        `Limit each stakeholder's speaking time at the reviews`,
        `Hold a separate review just for the department head`,
        `Meet the department head to learn what's behind it`],
  a:3, why:`Coming to every review is engagement, even when it's critical. A private conversation can find the interests behind the objections, and some may be worth adding to the backlog. Written objections are the near miss. They protect the review, but they keep the department head at arm's length without finding out what matters to them. Speaking limits treat the symptom, and a separate review splits stakeholders who need to hear each other.`,
  task:`P4`, domain:`People`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`The organization asks a project manager to mentor a new project manager from another department. What should they set up at their first meeting?`,
  opts:[`The mentee's goals and how often they'll meet`,
        `A list of projects the mentee should shadow`,
        `A schedule of the mentor's lessons learned talks`,
        `A review of the mentee's current project plans`],
  a:0, why:`Mentoring works best when it's built around the mentee's goals, with an agreed rhythm and expectations, and the mentee drives the topics. A review of current project plans is the near miss. It may be useful later, but starting there turns mentoring into supervision. A shadowing list and a series of talks are set by the mentor, not by the mentee's goals.`,
  task:`P5`, domain:`People`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A project manager with only predictive experience is assigned to a hybrid project. The organization has an agile community of practice with experienced coaches. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Plan the project the predictive way they know best`,
        `Take a weekend agile course before the project starts`,
        `Ask a coach from the community to mentor them`,
        `Let the agile teams run their parts with no input`],
  a:2, why:`Mentoring runs in every direction, and a good project manager seeks it out. A coach who has done this work can guide real decisions as they come up. The course is the near miss. Training helps, but one weekend can't answer the specific questions that come up on the project the way an ongoing mentor can. Falling back on predictive methods, or stepping away from the agile parts, both ignore the gap.`,
  task:`P5`, domain:`People`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`The customer service team expects a new self-service app to cut call volume in half. The approved business case says 15 percent. Launch is two months away. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Reconcile the two figures with the service team and sponsor`,
        `Let the service team find the real figure in the launch results`,
        `Send the service team a copy of the business case`,
        `Add features that might reach a 50 percent cut`],
  a:0, why:`A gap that big will look like failure on launch day unless it's dealt with now. Bringing the service team and the sponsor together to discuss the target aligns expectations, and it may turn up a reason for the higher figure. Sending the business case is the near miss. It states the official number, but a document doesn't explain why they expect more or get them to accept less. Waiting for results guarantees disappointment, and adding features chases a number nobody approved.`,
  task:`P5`, domain:`People`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`During planning interviews, the plant manager says production can't stop at all during the equipment upgrade. The draft plan includes a three-day shutdown. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Keep the shutdown in the plan and explain it later`,
        `Drop the shutdown and find a workaround during execution`,
        `Raise the conflict with the plant manager and sponsor now`,
        `Record the plant manager's view in the stakeholder register`],
  a:2, why:`Conflicting expectations found during planning are cheapest to resolve now. The project manager puts the expectation and the plan side by side with the people who can decide, and they may find another option, such as a weekend window or a phased cutover. Recording the view in the register is the near miss. Documenting expectations is part of the job, but it leaves the conflict open. Explaining later surprises the plant manager, and dropping the shutdown with no plan hides a real need.`,
  task:`P5`, domain:`People`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A product serves pharmacists, delivery drivers and patients. The product owner gets so many conflicting requests from each group that stakeholders feel ignored whatever the team builds. What could the project manager suggest?`,
  opts:[`Give each group an equal share of every sprint`,
        `Ask each group to pick one person to send requests`,
        `Rank requests by how many people asked for them`,
        `Build personas that group each type of user's needs`],
  a:3, why:`Personas turn many scattered requests into a few clear pictures of who the users are and what they need. They help the product owner explain choices and help stakeholders see where their needs fit. One contact per group is the near miss. It tidies the requests, but it doesn't help anyone weigh one group's needs against another's. Equal sprint shares ignore value, and counting requests rewards the loudest group.`,
  task:`P5`, domain:`People`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`Early in a project, the sponsor shares the project manager's rough order of magnitude estimate with the board as the project's committed cost. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Replace it right away with a definitive bottom up estimate`,
        `Pad the figure quietly with extra contingency`,
        `Let the board's figure stand and manage to it`,
        `Explain the estimate's range and when it will firm up`],
  a:3, why:`A rough order of magnitude estimate is wide on purpose, often minus 25 to plus 75 percent, and it narrows as planning goes on. The sponsor needs to know that, and when a firmer figure will exist, so the board's expectation matches reality. A definitive estimate right away is the near miss. The project doesn't have the detail to support one yet, and rushing it gives false precision. Quiet padding hides the truth, and letting the figure stand sets up a later surprise.`,
  task:`P5`, domain:`People`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A senior executive asks the agile team to demo each increment to her privately before the sprint review, so she can approve what other stakeholders see. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Hold the private demos to keep the executive satisfied`,
        `Cancel the sprint review and send her a summary`,
        `Explain the review's purpose and invite her input there`,
        `Ask the product owner to stop inviting other stakeholders`],
  a:2, why:`The sprint review exists so the team and its stakeholders inspect the increment together and adapt the backlog. A private approval step would filter what others see and slow feedback. Aligning the executive's expectations about how the review works, and giving her a real voice in it, meets her need for influence. Holding the private demos is the near miss. It keeps a powerful stakeholder happy, but it undermines the review for everyone else. The other two options shrink the review even further.`,
  task:`P5`, domain:`People`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`Two departments will share a new case management system. Each expects the system to follow its own workflow exactly, and the workflows differ in a dozen places. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Build both workflows into the system in full`,
        `Let the department with more users choose the workflow`,
        `Ask the vendor which workflow its other customers use`,
        `Run a workshop to agree on one shared workflow`],
  a:3, why:`Aligning expectations means bringing both sides together to find what they can share and where real differences need to stay. A facilitated workshop does that. Asking the vendor is the near miss. Industry practice is useful input, but it doesn't get these two departments to agree. Building both workflows doubles cost and complexity, and letting the larger group choose leaves the other one unaligned.`,
  task:`P5`, domain:`People`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`The sponsor hears a rumor that a key milestone will slip and calls the project manager, upset. The project manager knew about the slip two weeks ago. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Meet the sponsor to explain the slip and the options`,
        `Send the sponsor the updated schedule by email today`,
        `Explain that the slip was already in the risk register`,
        `Wait to go over it at the next steering meeting`],
  a:0, why:`The sponsor's trust depends on hearing bad news first from the project manager. The fix now is a direct conversation about what happened, the impact and the options, plus an agreement on how early warnings will reach the sponsor from now on. Sending the updated schedule is the near miss. The facts matter, but an email doesn't repair the surprise. Pointing to the risk register sounds defensive, and waiting makes it worse.`,
  task:`P6`, domain:`People`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`After a sprint review where the team demoed a clickable prototype, a sales director tells customers the product will launch next month. The real product is months away. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Ask the team to speed up to meet the sales director's date`,
        `Talk with the sales director today about the real status`,
        `Stop showing prototypes to sales at sprint reviews`,
        `Correct the date in the next stakeholder newsletter`],
  a:1, why:`The misunderstanding is spreading to customers, so it needs a prompt, direct conversation with the sales director to reset the expectation and agree what can be said. A correction in the newsletter is the near miss. It reaches people eventually, but too late and too indirectly to stop the promise to customers. Speeding up chases a date nobody planned, and hiding prototypes cuts off useful feedback.`,
  task:`P6`, domain:`People`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`A monthly pulse survey shows satisfaction among warehouse supervisors, a key user group, dropping for the third month in a row. Nothing in the project's own metrics has changed. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Add a question to the survey to find the cause`,
        `Meet some supervisors to learn what's behind the drop`,
        `Share the project's metrics with the supervisors`,
        `Ask the sponsor to reassure the supervisors`],
  a:1, why:`Monitoring engagement means acting on what the data shows. The supervisors know something the project's metrics don't capture, and talking with them finds out what. Adding a survey question is the near miss. It gathers more data, but it means another month's wait for a narrow answer to a problem that's already three months old. Sharing metrics or sending reassurance answers a question nobody has asked.`,
  task:`P6`, domain:`People`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`A regulator's inspector says they expect to witness every pressure test on a pipeline project. The plan assumed they'd witness a sample, and the test schedule is tight. What should the project manager do first?`,
  opts:[`Rework the test schedule to fit a witness at every test`,
        `Ask the sponsor to appeal to the inspector's supervisor`,
        `Run the tests as planned and offer the records later`,
        `Meet the inspector to agree on the witnessing approach`],
  a:3, why:`A regulator's changed expectation needs to be understood and agreed before the plan is redone. Meeting the inspector may show what's behind the request, which tests matter most, and what's flexible. Reworking the schedule is the near miss. It may be needed, but doing it before talking may build a plan around a misunderstanding. Going over the inspector's head or ignoring the request puts the project's approval at risk.`,
  task:`P6`, domain:`People`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A marketing manager sends the product owner new requests every few days and expects each one in the next sprint. Most aren't, and the manager is growing frustrated. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Ask the team to take one marketing request a sprint`,
        `Have the manager send requests to you instead`,
        `Tell the manager that sprint scope is fixed`,
        `Help the product owner show how the backlog is ordered`],
  a:3, why:`The manager's frustration comes from not seeing how requests are weighed. Showing the ordered backlog and how the product owner sets priority, and inviting the manager to refinement, resets the expectation without promising scope. Saying sprint scope is fixed is the near miss. It's true for the current sprint, but it doesn't explain why requests miss the next one too. A quota per sprint bypasses value, and routing through the project manager adds a layer without solving anything.`,
  task:`P6`, domain:`People`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`A hardware vendor's delay means a feature promised to the sales team won't be ready for a trade show in six weeks. A smaller version could be. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Tell the sales team now and offer the smaller version`,
        `Wait two weeks to see whether the vendor catches up`,
        `Ask the team to work overtime to finish the full feature`,
        `Let the sponsor decide when the sales team hears`],
  a:0, why:`Bad news delivered early, with options, keeps trust and gives sales time to plan around it. The smaller version is a real option to put in front of them. Letting the sponsor decide when to tell them is the near miss. The sponsor should know, but the people affected need to hear soon, and holding the news for the sponsor's call delays it. Waiting and hoping costs sales their planning time, and overtime can't speed up a vendor's hardware.`,
  task:`P6`, domain:`People`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`A department director has twice gone straight to the sponsor to object to project decisions, and nobody checked with the project manager beforehand. What's the project manager's best response?`,
  opts:[`Ask the sponsor to send the director back to you`,
        `Copy the director on every decision from now on`,
        `Report the director's behavior to their manager`,
        `Meet the director to understand the concerns`],
  a:3, why:`The director's concerns are strong enough to escalate, which is a sign of unmet expectations. A direct meeting to hear them, and to agree how concerns can reach the project manager, deals with the cause. Asking the sponsor to redirect the director is the near miss. It may cut down the escalations, but it doesn't address what the director is worried about. Copying them on every decision floods them, and reporting them damages the relationship.`,
  task:`P6`, domain:`People`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`In the bottling line case, what should the project manager do about the senior operators who plan to retire?`,
  opts:[`Ask them to write manuals before their last day`,
        `Pair them with newer operators before the move`,
        `Offer them a bonus to stay on through closeout`,
        `Hire experienced operators from another company`],
  a:1, why:`Much of what senior operators know is tacit, such as the feel of a machine that's about to jam, and it passes best by working side by side. They'll leave when the line moves, so the pairing has to happen now, on the old line. Writing manuals is the near miss. It captures explicit knowledge and is worth doing too, but on its own it misses what they know by feel. A bonus only delays the problem, and outside hires don't know these machines.`,
  task:`P7`, domain:`People`, approach:`predictive`, case:`m2c2`, cq:3 },

{ q:`An agile team's wiki is months out of date, and new members say they learn more by asking around than by reading it. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Schedule one sprint just for rewriting the wiki`,
        `Assign one member to keep the wiki up to date`,
        `Add doc updates to the team's definition of done`,
        `Replace the wiki with recorded video walkthroughs`],
  a:2, why:`Knowledge stays current when keeping it current is part of finishing the work. Putting doc updates in the definition of done builds that habit into every story. A sprint for rewriting is the near miss. It fixes the wiki once, but without a change in habits it drifts out of date again. A single keeper becomes a bottleneck, and recordings go stale just as fast.`,
  task:`P7`, domain:`People`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`A new engineer joins a hybrid project in its fourth month. What's the best way to help them understand the team's responsibilities and ways of working?`,
  opts:[`Send them the project plan and the team contact list`,
        `Let them pick up the norms by watching for a few weeks`,
        `Hold a team meeting where everyone restates their role`,
        `Give them a buddy and go over the team agreements`],
  a:3, why:`Onboarding works when someone explains how the team actually works, and the working agreements and responsibility assignments are the record of that. A buddy answers the questions documents can't. Sending the plan is the near miss. The new engineer needs it, but on its own it leaves out the unwritten norms. Learning by watching is slow and patchy, and a full meeting for one person takes everyone's time for less effect.`,
  task:`P7`, domain:`People`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`A project manager is starting to plan a data center move. The organization has done three similar moves in the last five years. What should the project manager do with that history?`,
  opts:[`Study those moves' lessons and talk to their managers`,
        `Reuse the last move's plan as the baseline for this one`,
        `Read the lessons only when problems come up`,
        `Ask the PMO for a summary of the moves later on`],
  a:0, why:`Lessons learned are worth something only if they're used, and the start of planning is when they help most. Reading them, and talking with the people who ran those moves, brings out what the documents leave out. Reusing the last plan is the near miss. It saves time, but every move differs, and copying the plan copies its mistakes without the reasons. Waiting for problems or a later summary loses the chance to plan around known traps.`,
  task:`P7`, domain:`People`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`An agile team is starting a year of work on a product with a lot of undocumented history. The project manager wants to know where losing one person would hurt most. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Ask everyone to document all of their work in the wiki`,
        `Map with the team which knowledge only one person holds`,
        `Review each member's skills profile from HR records`,
        `Wait until someone leaves, then plan the handover`],
  a:1, why:`Knowledge transfer starts with knowing which knowledge is critical and where it sits. Mapping it with the team, for example in a simple skills matrix that shows what only one person knows, tells the project manager where to pair, document or cross train first. Documenting everything is the near miss. It sounds thorough, but it spreads effort evenly instead of on the few areas that matter. HR profiles show formal skills, not project knowledge, and waiting for a departure leaves no time to act.`,
  task:`P7`, domain:`People`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`On a plant upgrade, two veteran technicians give vague answers when newer staff ask how the old controls work, and they keep their notes to themselves. A supervisor hears they're worried the new system will make them expendable. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Tell them to hand over their notes by the end of the week`,
        `Talk with them about the worry and the role they can play`,
        `Ask HR to add knowledge sharing to their performance goals`,
        `Have a junior engineer watch them and write down what they do`],
  a:1, why:`People share what they know when it feels safe to do so. The project manager talks with the technicians about their concern and shows how their knowledge matters, for example by asking them to lead the handover of the old controls and help shape how the new ones are run. Adding sharing to their performance goals is the near miss. It signals that sharing counts, but it doesn't touch the fear behind the silence and can make it worse. Ordering the notes handed over gets paper without the know-how, and quietly watching them confirms the fear.`,
  task:`P7`, domain:`People`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`Two members of a hybrid team each assumed the other was recording interface decisions. Three weeks of decisions were never written down. What should the project manager do to stop it happening again?`,
  opts:[`Agree with the team who records which decisions and where`,
        `Record every interface decision yourself from now on`,
        `Ask the two members to rebuild the missing record from memory`,
        `Add a decision log template to the project files`],
  a:0, why:`The gap came from unclear responsibilities, so the fix is a clear team agreement on who records which decisions and where they go. A template is the near miss. It gives the record a home, but it doesn't say who fills it in, and that's how the gap happened. Rebuilding the missing weeks is worth doing, but it catches up without preventing a repeat. Doing it yourself makes the project manager the bottleneck.`,
  task:`P7`, domain:`People`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`A project has 14 stakeholders, including the project manager. How many communication channels are there?`,
  opts:[`182 channels`,
        `196 channels`,
        `78 channels`,
        `91 channels`],
  a:3, why:`Channels are n times (n minus 1), divided by 2. Here that's 14 times 13 divided by 2, or 91. 182 is the near miss. It's the same formula without dividing by 2, which counts every channel twice. 196 is 14 squared, and 78 uses 13 people, leaving out the project manager.`,
  task:`P8`, domain:`People`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`Two developers on a distributed agile team have traded 40 chat messages over two days about an interface, and they still don't agree on what it should do. What should the project manager suggest?`,
  opts:[`A written summary from each developer of their position`,
        `A short video call between the two to talk it through`,
        `A post in the team channel asking others to weigh in`,
        `Waiting until the next sprint review to settle it`],
  a:1, why:`Complex, ambiguous topics need a rich, interactive channel where people can ask and answer in real time. A short call often settles in minutes what chat can't settle in days. Written summaries are the near miss. They clarify each position, but they keep the slow back and forth going. Opening it to the whole channel adds voices without adding clarity, and waiting for the review leaves the work blocked.`,
  task:`P8`, domain:`People`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`A new executive joins the steering committee of a hybrid program. Their assistant says they never read written reports and prefer a five-minute phone summary each week. The communications plan gives every member a written monthly report. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Keep sending the written report the plan calls for`,
        `Add a weekly call for this executive to the plan`,
        `Ask the assistant to read the report to the executive`,
        `Move every committee member to phone summaries`],
  a:1, why:`The communications plan exists to meet stakeholders' needs, and when a key stakeholder's needs differ, the plan changes to match. A short weekly call for this executive does that. Sticking with the report is the near miss. It follows the plan, but a report the executive won't read communicates nothing. Passing it through the assistant loses the conversation, and switching everyone ignores what the others prefer.`,
  task:`P8`, domain:`People`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`A section of a construction program's design standard has been found unsafe. Engineers at four sites must stop using it today. Which communication method fits best?`,
  opts:[`Pull communication through the shared standards site`,
        `A notice in next week's program newsletter`,
        `Push alerts to each site lead, with confirmation back`,
        `Interactive discussion at the monthly review call`],
  a:2, why:`Urgent information that people must act on today can't wait for them to look it up. Pushing it straight to each site lead, and asking each to confirm, makes sure it arrived and was understood. Updating the shared site is the near miss. The standard should change there too, but pull communication relies on people checking, and nobody knows to check. The newsletter and the monthly call come far too late.`,
  task:`P8`, domain:`People`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`An upcoming release will change how customers log in to a utility's website. What should the project manager do first about communication?`,
  opts:[`Work out who's affected and what each group needs`,
        `Email every customer the full details of the new login`,
        `Post a notice on the website on release day`,
        `Brief the call center after the release goes out`],
  a:0, why:`Communication planning starts with analyzing needs. Customers, the call center and others will each need different messages at different times, and methods and timing follow from that. Emailing every customer is the near miss. It may be part of the plan, but sending it before the analysis may leave out the call center and confuse customers who never use the website. A release day notice and a briefing after release both come too late.`,
  task:`P8`, domain:`People`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`After each sprint review, the project manager sends stakeholders notes on what was decided. Nobody replies, and two stakeholders later act as if a decision never happened. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Send the notes a second time a day later`,
        `Make the notes longer and more detailed`,
        `Ask the two stakeholders to read the notes more closely`,
        `Confirm the key decisions in the room before the review ends`],
  a:3, why:`Communication isn't done until it's understood. Playing back the decisions in the room and asking people to confirm them catches misunderstandings while everyone is there. Longer notes are the near miss. More detail can help, but if people aren't reading or absorbing the notes now, more words won't fix it. Sending them twice or telling people to read closely still assumes the message landed.`,
  task:`P8`, domain:`People`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`A key vendor's engineers read English as a second language. They've misread two long emails about test requirements, and the misreadings caused rework. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Send short points with diagrams and check understanding`,
        `Write longer emails that explain every detail`,
        `Ask the vendor to hire a translator to work on the project`,
        `Switch to phone calls for all of the test requirements`],
  a:0, why:`Clear communication fits the receiver. Short points with visuals, followed by a check that they were understood, leave less room for misreading. Phone calls are the near miss. Talking helps, but requirements need a written record, and spoken detail can be just as hard to follow. Longer emails give people more to misread, and a translator adds cost without changing how the messages are written.`,
  task:`P8`, domain:`People`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`A stakeholder asks for daily status updates. The communications plan says weekly, and producing a full report every day would take the team hours. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Send the full report daily as the stakeholder asked`,
        `Decline and point to the weekly reporting schedule in the plan`,
        `Find out what the stakeholder needs and agree on a format`,
        `Ask the sponsor whether daily reports are allowed`],
  a:2, why:`Behind a request for daily updates there's usually a specific worry, such as one milestone or one risk. Finding it lets the project manager meet the need, perhaps with a short daily note or a live dashboard, and update the plan. Declining is the near miss. The plan says weekly, but the plan serves stakeholders, not the other way round. Producing the full report daily wastes hours, and asking the sponsor skips the conversation.`,
  task:`P8`, domain:`People`, approach:`predictive` },

]});
